# WhatsApp Web (send)

Send a WhatsApp text message from the user's **already-connected** WhatsApp account.
Backed by `scripts/wa.js` (Baileys, WhatsApp Web multi-device).

⚠️ **Unofficial + risky.** Uses the unofficial WhatsApp Web protocol — against
WhatsApp's ToS; the linked number can be **banned**, especially for bulk sending.
Only send to recipients the user explicitly named. Never mass-send; if asked to blast
many numbers, warn and confirm first.

> **Connecting is NOT done here.** Pairing/QR happens ONLY on the dedicated
> **Connect WhatsApp** screen in the app. This skill must **never** run `link` or show
> a QR in chat. It only checks connection and sends.

## When to Use
The user asks to send/deliver a WhatsApp message to **exactly ONE recipient**. They give
ONLY the **recipient number** (full international format e.g. `91XXXXXXXXXX`) and the
**message text**. The **sender is always the user's connected WhatsApp account** — never
ask for, expect, or pass a "from" number; the skill always sends from whatever WhatsApp is
connected.

> **⛔ Multiple recipients? Do NOT use this skill.** If the user names **2 or more**
> numbers, gives a list, or says "bulk", STOP and use the **`send-whatsapp-bulk`** skill
> instead — it stages all numbers at once and dispatches them in the background with proper
> pacing, cooldown, and DB logging. Looping this single-send skill per number is wrong:
> it's slow (a synchronous send + a model turn each) and skips all the safety pacing.

## Setup (once)
Skill dir: `$HERMES_HOME/skills/whatsapp-web`. Set a shorthand and ensure deps exist:
```bash
WA="$HERMES_HOME/skills/whatsapp-web"
[ -d "$WA/node_modules" ] || (cd "$WA" && npm install --omit=dev --no-audit --no-fund)
```

## Procedure

### 1. Check the connection FIRST (gate) — always
```bash
node "$WA/scripts/wa.js" status
```
Read the JSON's **`connected`** boolean:
- **`connected: false`** (or an `error`/`not_linked`) → **STOP. Do NOT send, do NOT
  pair, do NOT run `link` or show a QR.** Reply with exactly the *Not-connected reply*
  below and end your turn.
- **`connected: true`** → continue to step 2.

### 2. Send (only when connected) — always from the connected account
```bash
node "$WA/scripts/wa.js" send --to <TO> --text "<MESSAGE>"
```
**Never pass `--from`** — the skill sends from the connected WhatsApp automatically.
On `{"ok":true,"to":"…","id":"…","from":"…"}` confirm to the user (name the recipient;
the `from` in the result is the connected number). On error, report the `error` field
and what it means (see Pitfalls).

## Not-connected reply (use verbatim when `connected` is false)
> Your WhatsApp isn't connected to MoneyShot yet, so I can't send this. Connect it
> here: **[Connect WhatsApp](https://ultron.lsnw.io/moneyshot/?connect=whatsapp)** —
> or click the circle in the top-right corner and choose **Connect WhatsApp**, then
> scan the QR code. Once connected, ask me again and I'll send it.

## Pitfalls
- `not_linked` / `connected:false` → not connected; give the *Not-connected reply*. Never pair here.
- `need_from` → more than one WhatsApp account is connected (unusual). Do NOT ask the user for a "from" number; tell them to remove the extra connection on the Connect WhatsApp screen so exactly one remains, then retry.
- `recipient_not_on_whatsapp` → the `--to` number has no WhatsApp account (check the country code).
- `logged_out` → WhatsApp invalidated/unlinked the session; treat as not connected → *Not-connected reply*.
- Message text with quotes/newlines: pass it as a single quoted `--text` argument.

## Verification
A successful send prints `{"ok":true,"to":"…","id":"…","from":"…"}`. Connection can be
re-checked any time with `node "$WA/scripts/wa.js" status` → `"connected":true`.
